ing-papers/survey-measures-natural-rate-interest">https://www.mercatus.org/research/working-papers/survey-measures-natural-rate-interest</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/04/saturday-assorted-links-505.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=saturday-assorted-links-505">Saturday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://www.mercatus.org/research/working-papers/survey-measures-natural-rate-interest</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.mercatus.org/research/working-papers/survey-measures-natural-rate-interest</guid><pubDate>Sat, 12 Apr 2025 16:19:18 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Jon Hartley (@Jon_Hartley_) on X]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/GoHJmA0WYAAMpoY.jpg:large"></p><p>Jon Hartley (@Jon_Hartley_) on X</p><p>New paper on ***Survey R-star***, which we need to all start using. Model-based r-star (eg Laubach-Williams) have massive standard errors. Instead we should increasingly use median survey estimates from recently developed central bank surveys🧵
